You are running the `/release` skill for the whygraph project.

Your job is to bump the version, commit, tag, and push — preparing the repo for a GitHub Release.

## Step 1 — Ask the user

Ask exactly this question and wait for their answer:

> Is this a **patch** (bug fix), **minor** (new feature), or **major** (breaking change) release?

Accept any of: `patch`, `minor`, `major`, `p`, `m`, `maj` (map shorthand to full type).

## Step 2 — Verify working tree is clean

Run:
```bash
git status --porcelain
```

If there are uncommitted changes, stop and tell the user:
> Working tree has uncommitted changes. Please commit or stash them before releasing.

## Step 3 — Verify tests pass

Run:
```bash
npx vitest run
```

If any test fails, stop and tell the user which tests failed. Do not proceed.

## Step 4 — Bump the version

Run:
```bash
npm version <type> --no-git-tag-version
```

This updates `package.json` only. Then read the new version:
```bash
node -p "require('./package.json').version"
```

## Step 5 — Commit, tag, and push

```bash
git add package.json
git commit -m "chore: release v<version>"
git tag v<version>
git push origin main
git push origin v<version>
```

## Step 6 — Report and instruct

Tell the user:
- The new version (e.g. `v0.3.0`)
- That the tag has been pushed
- Exactly what to do next:

> **Next step:** Go to https://github.com/geovanie-ruiz/whygraph/releases/new, select tag `v<version>`, write a changelog, and click **Publish release**. GitHub Actions will run the full test suite and publish to npm automatically.
